+**The code default is overridden by the engine's spec cfgs.** `sys_spec_PostProcessing`
+(`Engine/Config/CVarGroups/sys_spec_PostProcessing.cfg` line 21, `r_SunShafts=2` in its
+`[default]` block, packed in `engine.pak`) is applied at startup and puts the cvar back to 2
+whatever `RendererCVars.cpp` says - the editor log's `[CVARS] MISSING ... sys_spec_*` lines are
+that system at work. (`mobile.cfg:78` and `recording.cfg:62` set it too, for their own profiles.)
+Two consequences:
+
+* **The Cinematic Camera turns the shafts off itself** (2026-09-09, second pass). While the
+  component drives the render state it publishes `SunShafts_Active = 0` on the post-effect bus
+  every frame after the Time of Day update - the other input of the stage's gate - and gives the
+  TOD's value back on deactivate. The log prints `[CinematicCamera] sun shafts off (retired in
+  favour of volumetric fog)` once per activation. It does not write `r_sunshafts` (VF_DUMPTODISK,
+  global; `decisions/s0-master-switch.md`), so `r_sunshafts` may still read 2 in the console while
+  the stage is off - expected. With no cinematic camera active, the cfg's value rules.
+* **To make the default stick engine-wide**, edit that cfg line (the sys_spec system itself is
+  still to be researched).
+
 Why retired: a screen-space shaft is a radial blur of bright pixels with no scattering term, no
 occluder test and no path length; every bound and condition the scene-referred work added to it
 (`decisions/s2-sun-shafts.md`, `decisions/s8-sun-shafts-condition.md`) was a patch on an
@@ -741,14 +758,21 @@ map included, and once S8 put it on scene units it became the correct source of
 | DLL | CryRenderD3D11 |
 | Purpose | Divisor from screen resolution to the froxel grid's width and height (minimum 2; depth is `r_VolumetricFogTexDepth`, 32). 6 instead of 10 gives (10/6)^2 = **2.8x the froxels** - at 1080p a 320x180x32 volume instead of 192x108x32 - so shaft edges stop smearing across the sky and the temporal reprojection has less to hide. Cost scales with the froxel count in every fog pass (injection, in-scattering, integration): budget about 2.8x the stock fog time, which on the target hardware is still well under a millisecond. Set 10 back if the fog is the frame's bottleneck. |
 
